INT. PLANETARIUM DOME - NIGHT

Empty rows of recliners. The ceiling swims with painted stars. A projector the size of a dumbbell hums at the center.

ARCHIE SOLOMONS, 40s, black turtleneck, wire cutters, hangs upside down from a catwalk and works on a panel. BILLIE CROSS, 30s, bandana, pushes a cart of equipment between the seats.

ARCHIE
Billie. Light.

BILLIE
Archie, we're in a planetarium.

ARCHIE
So?

BILLIE
So it's a room full of light.

ARCHIE
Not that kind. Flashlight.

She tosses one up. He catches it with his teeth.

BILLIE
Gross.

ARCHIE
(mumbling around it)
Efficient.

He yanks a wire. The stars flicker. The dome goes dark for an instant, then returns as a different sky.

BILLIE
That's not our constellation.

ARCHIE
It's Orion's cousin.

BILLIE
Orion doesn't have cousins.

ARCHIE
Everybody has cousins, Billie.

She rolls the cart under the projector. Pops its lid. Inside, a velvet-lined case. Inside the case: a glittering meteorite, black as oil.

BILLIE
There she is.

ARCHIE
Don't breathe on it.

BILLIE
I'm not breathing.

ARCHIE
You're breathing loudly.

She holds her breath. Slides the rock into a lead bag. The dome shivers. Music swells. The stars begin to wheel on a timer.

They freeze. Archie drops from the catwalk. Lands in a crouch.

ARCHIE
Out. Now.

BILLIE
Archie.

ARCHIE
What?

BILLIE
That was only the nine o'clock show on a timer.

ARCHIE
Oh.
